<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.2.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00384</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.2.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c08704af-7faf-4797-874a-525d9c40af2f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the following points is the solution to the system of equations that is graphed?
  <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f8b2817150ab876b42893d7d192fa7208d9b2dfc"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \((20, 0) \)</item>
  <item>\( (0, 20) \)</item>
  <item>\( (45, 0) \)</item>
  <item>\( (5, 15) \)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((5, 15)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the following points is the solution to the system of equations that is graphed?
  <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/33de84819a3f6bb93578fdc791adb33bc752fc40"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \((5, 2)\)</item>
  <item>\( (2, 5) \)</item>
  <item>\( (8, 0) \)</item>
  <item>\( (5.2, 0)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((5, 2)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>What two equations are represented by the graph?  Select <emphasis>two</emphasis>.
  <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8bbab63874107229f204df779b103cf9cc8e4dce"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(y=\frac12x-1\)</item>
  <item>\(y=4x-2\)</item>
  <item>\(y=\frac14x+2\)</item>
  <item>\(y=\frac{-1}{2}x+1\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Correct! The system of equations includes \(y=\frac12x-1\) and \(y=4x-2\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> What two equations are represented by the graph?  Select<emphasis> two</emphasis>.
  <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/475020aab78e3fc53257a802ab6832ed21f34470"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(y=x-1\)</item>
  <item>\(y=x+1\)</item>
  <item>\(y=\frac13x+2\)</item>
  <item> \(y=3x-2\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Correct! The system of equations includes \(y=x-1\)  and \(y=3x-2\).</para>
<para>For questions 5–8, use the following scenario and matching table.</para>
<para><quote>A streaming service has two different membership plans. Option A charges $10 per month plus $5 for each movie. Option B charges $20 a month then $2 a movie.</quote></para><table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis><para>Number of movies in a month, \(x\).</para></emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis><para>0</para></emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis><para>1</para></emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis><para>2</para></emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis><para>3</para></emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis><para>4</para></emphasis></entry></row>
        </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis><para>Option A</para></emphasis></entry><entry><para>10</para></entry>
      <entry><para>15</para></entry>
      <entry><para>20</para></entry>
      <entry><para>25</para></entry>
      <entry><para>30</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis><para>Option B</para></emphasis></entry><entry><para>20</para></entry>
      <entry><para>22</para></entry>
      <entry><para>24</para></entry>
      <entry><para>26</para></entry>
      <entry><para>28</para></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the \(y\)-intercept for Option A and Option B? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>Option A: 10 
  Option B: 20</item>
  <item>Option A:  20 
  Option B: 10</item>
  <item>Option A: 5 
  Option B: 2</item>
  <item>Option A: 2 
  Option B: 5</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Correct! The  \(y\)-intercepts are Option A = 10 and Option B = 20.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the slope of Option A and Option B? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>Option A: \(m =\frac 15\) 
  Option B: \(m =\frac 12\)</item>
  <item>Option A:  \(m=2\)  
  Option B: \(m =5\) </item>
  <item>Option A: \(m =\frac12\) 
  Option B: \(m =\frac15\)</item>
  <item>Option A: \(m =5\) 
  Option B: \(m =2\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Correct! The slopes are Option A = 5 and Option B = 2.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the equation of Option A?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(y=10x+5\)</item>
  <item>\(y=5x-10\)</item>
  <item>\(y=5x+10\)</item>
  <item>\(y=10x-5\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Correct!  The equation of the line for Option A is \(y=5x+10\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the slope for Option B?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(y=5x+20\)</item>
  <item>\(y=2x+20\)</item>
  <item>\(y=5x+10\)</item>
  <item>\(y=2x+10\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Correct!  The equation of the line for Option B is \(y=2x+20\).</para>
<para>For questions 9 - 12, use the following situation and graph.</para>
<para><quote>A family buys a total of 32 tickets at a carnival. Ride tickets cost $1.50 each and food tickets cost $3.25 each. The family pays a total of $90 for the tickets. The situation is graphed below.</quote><quote><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/eb44767d0c6710228091a100debe7800c1ed2a24"/></media></figure></quote><quote>The \(x\)-axis represents the number of ride tickets and the \(y\)-axis represents the number of food tickets.</quote></para><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which line depicts the equation that represents the statement “A family buys a total of 32 tickets at a carnival”?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> the orange, solid line </item>
  <item> the blue, dashed line </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The orange, solid line. It represents \(x + y = 32\).</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>The remaining line represents the statements “Ride tickets cost $1.50 each and food tickets cost $3.25 each. The family pays a total of $90 for the tickets.” Which equation corresponds to these statements and the remaining line?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(1.50x + 3.25y = 90\) </item>
  <item> \(3.25x + 1.50y = 90\) </item>
  <item> \((1.50 + 3.25)x = 90y\) </item>
  <item> \((1.50 + 3.25)y = 90x\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The blue, dashed line represents the equation \(1.50x + 3.25y = 90\). This equation also corresponds to the scenario's statements.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the approximate number of ride tickets the family purchased?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> 32 </item>
  <item> 60 </item>
  <item> 8 </item>
  <item> 24 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 8</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="12" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the approximate number of food tickets the family purchased?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> 32 </item>
  <item> 60 </item>
  <item> 7 </item>
  <item> 24 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 24</para>
</section>
